<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Syr Darya</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Syr Darya is a major river of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> that flows from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tian-shan">Tian Shan</a> mountains north-westwards to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aral-sea">Aral Sea</a>. Known to classical writers as the Jaxartes, it marked an important frontier between the settled lands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transoxiana">Transoxiana</a> and the steppe. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-turkic-khaganate">Second Turkic Khaganate</a> under Qapaghan extended its influence as far as the Syr Darya in the early eighth century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/syr-darya.jpg" alt="Syr Darya" width="320" height="170" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Petar Milošević · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Syr_Darya.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Syr Darya rises in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tian-shan">Tian Shan</a> mountains, where its two headstreams, the Naryn and the Kara Darya, come together in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fergana-valley">Fergana Valley</a>, and flows west and north-west through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a>, the Sughd province of Tajikistan and southern Kazakhstan to the northern remnants of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aral-sea">Aral Sea</a>. With the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxus">Amu Darya</a> it is one of the two main rivers of the Aral Sea basin. It drains an area of over 800,000 square kilometres, though no more than 160,000 contribute significant flow, and its annual flow is about half that of the Amu Darya.</p><p>Along its course the river irrigates the most productive agricultural regions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, and canals of central importance in this arid region were built and maintained by local governments through history. Many fell into disuse in the 17th and early 18th century, and the Khanate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kokand">Kokand</a> rebuilt many in the 19th.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The earliest recorded name is the Greek Jaxartes, which echoes the Old Persian Yakhsha Arta, perhaps meaning true pearl and referring to the colour of its glacial water. Until the Arab conquest the river's Turkic name was Yinçü ögüz, the pearl river. The present name Syr does not appear before the 16th century; in Tajik it means secret.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> reached the Jaxartes in 329 BCE, after travelling through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a> and Sogdia without opposition. In October of that year the Macedonians fought the Battle of Jaxartes against the Saka and killed some 1,200 combatants, including the nomads' leader. Alexander placed a garrison in the City of Cyrus and renamed it <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria-eschate">Alexandria Eschate</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> the furthest; since at least the Muslim conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> the city has been called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khujand">Khujand</a>. Revolt then spread through Sogdia and brought two years of warfare. In the mid-19th century, during the Russian conquest of Turkestan, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> introduced steam navigation on the river.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Soviet irrigation projects around the Syr Darya and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxus">Amu Darya</a> diverted their water to farmland, so that in some periods none reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aral-sea">Aral Sea</a>, once the world's fourth-largest lake. The Aral Sea virtually disappeared in the post-Soviet era.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Syr Darya” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Around 710, Göktürk armies led by Tonyukuk and Kül Tigin crushed the Yenisei Kyrgyz and killed their qaghan, and campaigns soon carried Göktürk influence from Manchuria to the <b>Syr Darya</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gokturk-empire">Gokturk Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Most of the Uzbek nomads later followed Abu'l-Khayr's grandson Muhammad Shaybani south into Transoxiana, and the Kazakhs became masters of most of the Dasht-i Qipchaq, the great steppe north of the <b>Syr Darya</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kazakh-khanate">Kazakh Khanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The empire reached its height under Ala ad-Din Muhammad II (r. 1200–1220), who overthrew the Ghurids in Afghanistan and the Qara Khitai in Transoxiana and ruled from western Iran to the <b>Syr Darya</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/khwarazmian-empire">Khwarazmian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…BCE distinguish several groups: the Saka Tigraxauda, 'with pointed caps', probably east of the Caspian and along the <b>Syr Darya</b>; the Saka Haumavarga, probably in Fergana and the Pamirs, whose name is usually linked to the ritual drink hauma; and the Saka Paradraya, 'beyond the sea', the Scythians…</blockquote><a href="/empire/saka">Saka · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The dynasty took its name from Seljuk Beg, a chieftain of the Qiniq clan of the Oghuz who, traditionally around 985 CE, converted to Sunni Islam and led his followers to the lower <b>Syr Darya</b> near Jand, on the frontiers of the Samanid and later Ghaznavid spheres.</blockquote><a href="/empire/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The land between the Amu Darya and <b>Syr Darya</b> was settled by Scythian nomads, and its cities, Bukhara and Samarkand, grew rich as Sogdian merchants carried the silk trade.</blockquote><a href="/empire/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
